<section class="section"><div class="container prose"><h2>Why the two-year point is not the whole answer</h2><p>The current immigration framework includes <strong>five forms of arraigo</strong>: second opportunity, sociolaboral, social, socioformative and family. For the forms that require a residence period, two years is the general threshold in the current framework; family arraigo follows a different rule.</p><p>The practical review should cover how residence can be evidenced, the applicant’s circumstances, family situation, employment or training links where relevant, and any previous administrative action.</p><p><a href="https://www.boe.es/eli/es/rd/2024/11/19/1155/con" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the consolidated Immigration Regulation at the BOE →</a></p><p><a href="https://www.boe.es/eli/es/rd/2026/04/14/316" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">See the 2026 regulatory amendment at the BOE →</a></p><div class="callout"><strong>Important:</strong> meeting a residence threshold does not automatically mean that any form of arraigo is available. The relevant route and the remaining requirements must be checked together.</div></div></section>
<CTA title="Need to know which route fits?" text="Tell us how long you have been in Spain, your current status and what you need to achieve." label="Contact the firm" />
